# Django patterns

Narrows `python`'s design guidance to idiomatic Django (5.x) ORM, view, and
migration patterns and the anti-patterns they replace. Applies to Django
project files; a non-Django module in the same repo still follows `python`'s
own `rules/patterns.mdc`.

## Query optimization (the N+1 problem)

- When a view or serializer will access a related object for every row in a
  queryset (`obj.author.name` in a loop, or a DRF nested serializer field),
  add `select_related("author")` for a `ForeignKey`/`OneToOneField` and
  `prefetch_related("tags")` for a `ManyToManyField`/reverse `ForeignKey` —
  `select_related` joins in one query; `prefetch_related` issues one extra
  query per relation and joins in Python. Combine both when a
  `prefetch_related` path itself needs a `select_related` (`Prefetch("best_pizza__toppings")`
  after `select_related("best_pizza")`).
- Use `only()`/`defer()` to trim columns from a wide model only when a
  profiled query shows the extra columns matter; reaching for it by default
  makes the queryset fragile to a later field access that silently triggers
  a new query.
- Prefer `QuerySet.exists()` over `bool(qs)`/`len(qs) > 0` for an
  existence check, and `.count()` over `len(list(qs))`, so the database does
  the work instead of materializing every row into Python.
- Use `bulk_create`/`bulk_update`/`update()` for a batch write instead of a
  Python loop calling `.save()` per instance — a per-instance loop is an N+1
  on the write side, and skips `bulk_create`'s single round trip (note it
  bypasses `save()`/signals unless explicitly configured to call them).

## Migrations

- Run `python manage.py makemigrations` to generate a migration from a model
  change; never hand-write a migration's `operations` list to match a model
  edit by guesswork — let Django diff the models, then review the generated
  file.
- Give a migration that needs to transform existing row data a `RunPython`
  operation with both a forward and a reverse function (or
  `migrations.RunPython.noop` when reversal is genuinely not meaningful),
  not a schema-only migration that silently leaves old data inconsistent
  with a new constraint.
- Keep a destructive migration (dropping a column/table another release
  still reads) behind a deploy sequence that ships the code change first,
  then the schema change, when the project deploys code and migrations
  separately — a same-release drop-and-read is a two-phase problem, not a
  one-step migration.
- Never edit a migration file that has already been applied in another
  environment (shared branch, staging, production) to "fix" it in place;
  add a new migration instead — editing history desyncs any environment
  that already recorded the old migration as applied.

## Models as the single source of truth

- Put a field-level invariant (a `CheckConstraint`, a `unique_together`/
  `UniqueConstraint`, a `validators=[...]` list) on the model field or
  `Meta.constraints`, not only in a form's `clean()` — validation the
  database itself does not enforce can still be violated by a direct
  `.save()`, the admin, a shell, or another service writing to the same
  table.
- Use a model `Manager`/`QuerySet` subclass to name a repeated filter
  (`Order.objects.pending()`) instead of repeating the same `.filter(...)`
  chain at every call site.

## Views

- A view that only reads and renders/serializes belongs on a generic
  class-based view (`ListView`/`DetailView`/DRF `ReadOnlyModelViewSet`); a
  view with custom branching logic per HTTP method is a legitimate reason to
  drop to a function-based view or override a specific CBV method
  (`get_queryset`, `form_valid`) rather than reimplementing the whole
  request/response cycle.
- Return the framework's own response helpers (`HttpResponseRedirect`/
  `redirect()`, `JsonResponse`, DRF's `Response`) instead of building a raw
  `HttpResponse` with a hand-set `Content-Type` and manually serialized body.

## Anti-patterns to flag, not introduce

- A `for` loop over a queryset that touches a related object per iteration
  with no `select_related`/`prefetch_related` upstream — this is Django's
  most common performance bug and is invisible in a small dev dataset.
- `fields = "__all__"` on a `ModelForm`/`ModelSerializer` that accepts
  user input, silently exposing every future field for mass-assignment.
- A signal handler (`post_save`, etc.) that performs a slow or
  externally-visible side effect (sending an email, calling a third-party
  API) synchronously inside the request/response cycle instead of queuing it
  through the project's async task runner (Celery or equivalent).
